    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d1507">

                <pb facs="1216" n="1100" sameAs="frus:frus1912/pg1100"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 817.51/451.</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The American Minister to the Secretary of
                    State</hi>.</head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 35.]</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">American Legation</hi>, <lb/>
                            <hi rendition="#italic">Managua</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1912-05-17">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">May 17, 1912</hi></date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: Referring to my telegram of May 11, 11
                        a.m.,<anchor n="1" xml:id="d1507-f1"/> I have the honor to enclose herewith
                    a copy and translation of the measure<anchor n="1" xml:id="d1507-f2"/> passed by
                    the National Assembly authorizing the President of Nicaragua to make a
                    settlement of the Ethelburga loan.</p>
                <p>In that same connection I enclose a copy<anchor n="1" xml:id="d1507-f3"/> of a
                    telegram given to me by the President, the English text of which is probably
                    already before the Department. It is dated May 12, 1912, addressed by Brown
                    Brothers to their local representative, and contains the terms of the Ethelburga
                    settlement, as reported in my telegram of May 14, 4 p.m.<anchor n="1"
                        xml:id="d1507-f4"/>
                </p>
                <p>I have [etc.]</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">George T. Weitzel.</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
